c(3001,[3003],[(99,'24-27'),(105,'139-179')],'Nombres de Betti virtuels de Serre',
 'For separated finite-type complex varieties, alternating dimensions of weight-graded compactly supported cohomology are additive under a closed/open decomposition. This is the modern reconstruction of the named programme, not a formula fully written on scan99.',
 'open on the pages','B','Deligne Hodge III, 8.2.6 and 8.2.8, supplies functorial mixed Hodge structures and the compact-support exact sequence; strictness gives the reconstructed additivity. The attribution to Serre is retained without asserting an unverified first announcement.','Published 1974; first circulation of this exact formulation not established.','Established in the stated characteristic-zero realization; it does not construct an unconditional motivic level filtration in arbitrary characteristic.',['Deligne1974HodgeIII'],['S01','S03'])
c(3002,[3005],[(99,'24'),(106,'187-210')],'quotient ... poids i',
 'For proper complex X, H^i(X) has weights at most i and its weight-i quotient embeds into H^i of a smooth proper hypercover component. A single resolution detects that quotient, not all of H^i.',
 'proof sketched','B','Hodge III, 8.2.4–8.2.5, states the proper weight range and identifies the kernel of pullback to a resolution with the lower-weight part. Weil II, 3.3.8, supplies the finite-field pure-image version.','1974 Hodge III; 1980 Weil II.','Established with the precise realization and properness hypotheses.',['Deligne1974HodgeIII','Deligne1980WeilII'],['S01','S03','S08'])
c(3003,[3006],[(101,'71-99')],'inj?',
 'Pullback to normalization need not inject compactly supported cohomology of a singular curve. Let X be two affine lines meeting transversally at one point and Y their disjoint normalization. Then H_c^1(X)=Q_l and H_c^1(Y)=0.',
 'open on the pages','B','The normalization and closed/open exact sequences answer the written question negatively. Hodge III 8.2.8 and Weil II 3.3 provide the established weight/exact-sequence framework; the explicit computation is independently redone here.','Framework published 1974/1980; elementary example independently checked in this run.','The injectivity hope fails, while the top-weight quotient assertion C3002 survives.',['Deligne1974HodgeIII','Deligne1980WeilII'],['S01','S03','S08'],'programs/check_calculations.py; outputs/calculations.json: two-lines normalization/cochain calculation; proofs.md §1')
c(3004,[3007],[(104,'102-124')],'H¹ ... poids0,1,2; H² ...0,1,2,3,4',
 'For finite-type X the proposed low-degree ordinary-cohomology weight ranges are H^1:0,1,2 and H^2:0,...,4, with Hodge-level triangular bounds. Geometric motivic level is a stronger notion than Hodge level.',
 'stated without proof','C','Hodge III 8.2.4 proves weight/Hodge-type bounds for separated complex varieties; 8.3.7 extends the mixed Hodge construction beyond separated schemes. Weil II supplies numerical finite-field weights. Neither theorem identifies the manuscript geometric-level category with Hodge level.','1974 and 1980.','Numerical realization bounds established; the categorical level identification remains conditional and is not replaced by these references.',['Deligne1974HodgeIII','Deligne1980WeilII','Grothendieck1969HodgeFalse'],['S01','S03','S08','S17'])
c(3005,[3007],[(104,'112-122'),(114,'447')],'semi-simples',
 'Purity alone does not imply arithmetic semisimplicity of an arbitrary lisse Q_l-sheaf. On SpecF_q take Frobenius to act by a nonidentity unipotent 2×2 matrix; every eigenvalue is1, hence pointwise weight0, but the representation is not semisimple.',
 'false as stated (under the arbitrary arithmetic-purity reading)','C','Weil II 3.4.1(iii) and 3.4.12 give geometric semisimplicity, not arithmetic semisimplicity of every pure representation. The displayed counterexample does not refute the separate semisimplicity conjecture for smooth proper motivic cohomology.','Weil II published 1980.','Keep geometric theorem, arithmetic counterexample, and motivic semisimplicity conjecture separate. The source context may intend the last, so no unconditional author-error attribution is made.',['Deligne1980WeilII'],['S08','S09'],'programs/check_calculations.py: unipotent matrix family; proofs.md §2')
c(3006,[3008],[(104,'127')],'extensions ... possible ici?',
 'For lisse pointwise pure sheaves F,G on a smooth finite-type finite-field scheme, nonzero Ext^1(F,G) requires wt(F)−wt(G) integral and wt(F)≥wt(G); a geometrically nonsplit extension requires strict inequality. The manuscript weights are integral; arithmetic extensions of equal weights can persist.',
 'open on the pages','B','Weil II 3.4.3–3.4.4 gives the exact order of weights and geometric/arithmetic distinction. Equal-weight arithmetic self-extensions are not killed by purity.','1980.','The appropriate wrong-order extension vanishing is established; replacing geometric splitting by arithmetic semisimplicity is invalid.',['Deligne1980WeilII'],['S08','S09'],'Unipotent equal-weight boundary case checked in outputs/calculations.json.')
c(3007,[3009],[(105,'168-180'),(106,'187-210')],'Proposition1',
 'For smooth separated n-dimensional X with constant coefficients, H_c^i has weights between max(0,2(i−n)) and i. Its weight/Hodge-type level is at most min(i,2n−i); the geometric-level interpretation requires the manuscript motivic inputs.',
 'proof sketched','C','Weil II 3.3.3 proves the finite-field lower range and 3.3.4 the compact upper range; Hodge III 8.2.4/8.2.6 gives the complex Hodge bounds. The geometric-level assertion is not silently inferred from Hodge type.','1974/1980.','The realization bounds are theorems; categorical level remains under the stated motivic hypotheses.',['Deligne1974HodgeIII','Deligne1980WeilII'],['S01','S03','S08'])
c(3008,[3009],[(105,'178-180')],'les deux dernières pièces ...2n−i−1',
 'If the surviving phrase includes both highest weight pieces, its strict level improvement is false: for an elliptic curve n=i=1, the top weight piece H^1 has Hodge level1, not at most0.',
 'false as stated (literal two-piece reading)','F','Hodge III proves the ordinary Hodge bounds, and the elliptic example identifies the numerical failure. Codex K12-07 has alternative readings involving the second piece; without scans the intended exact quantifier cannot be identified.','1974 framework; textual issue unresolved 2026.','Scan alarm, not a settled correction of the author: the false literal reading and a potentially true narrower reading remain distinct.',['Deligne1974HodgeIII'],['S01','S03'],'programs/check_calculations.py: elliptic endpoint; proofs.md §3')
c(3009,[3010],[(105,'152-166'),(106,'185')],'dégénère ... E₂',
 'For the smooth proper simple-normal-crossings compactification used in the source, the weight spectral sequence has pure terms of the displayed weight on its first page, hence differentials of incompatible weights vanish after the first differential.',
 'proof sketched','B','Hodge III constructs the weight spectral sequence and Weil II establishes the purity input over finite fields. The E2 degeneration is for this weight-indexed spectral sequence; it is not degeneration of every local Leray sequence.','1974/1980.','Established with the specified compactification and indexing.',['Deligne1974HodgeIII','Deligne1980WeilII'],['S01','S03','S08'])
c(3010,[3009],[(106,'212-215')],'par dualité',
 'For smooth separated n-dimensional X, ordinary H^i has weights in [i,min(2i,2n)] and Hodge level at most min(i,2n−i), by duality from constant-coefficient compact support.',
 'proof sketched','C','Weil II 3.3.5 and 3.3.9 and Hodge III 8.2.4/8.2.6 establish the realization statements. Geometric level requires the additional source convention and motivic inputs.','1974/1980.','Realization bounds established; no theorem about algebraicity of Hodge subobjects is implied.',['Deligne1974HodgeIII','Deligne1980WeilII'],['S01','S03','S08'])
c(3011,[3009],[(107,'235-241')],'cas non lisse',
 'For separated finite-type n-dimensional X, H_c^i has weights in [0,i] and the stated numerical/Hodge-level restrictions obtained by stratifying into smooth pieces. Ordinary lower weights can drop below i on singular X.',
 'proof sketched','C','Weil II 3.3.4 and Hodge III 8.2.6–8.2.8 give compact-support mixedness and exact-sequence induction. Geometric level is a separate conditional assertion.','1974/1980.','The numerical realization statement survives singularities; source-level algebraic generation must remain explicit.',['Deligne1974HodgeIII','Deligne1980WeilII'],['S01','S03','S08'])
c(3012,[3009],[(107,'243-251')],'cas général',
 'For separated finite-type X of dimension n, ordinary H^i has weights in [0,min(2i,2n)] and Hodge level at most min(i,n). These do not equal the stronger smooth bound min(i,2n−i).',
 'proof sketched','B','Hodge III 8.2.4 gives the mixed Hodge type bounds; its descent construction explains the singular case. Weil II gives the corresponding numerical weight formalism.','1974/1980.','Established as a realization statement with the exact hypotheses; no smoothness is silently added.',['Deligne1974HodgeIII','Deligne1980WeilII'],['S01','S03','S08'])
c(3013,[3012],[(107,'226-232')],'niveau ...≤2n−i?',
 'The source asks for a stronger ordinary-cohomology level estimate in the nonseparated setting, while noting that its compact-support formalism is unavailable there. The exact geometric-level convention is essential.',
 'open on the pages','C','Hodge III 8.3.7 constructs mixed Hodge structures for nonseparated schemes via étale hypercovers. It does not by itself prove the manuscript geometric-level bound; the searched modern dimension-filtration paper concerns a different triangulated category.','1974; comparison paper first2016/published2018.','Partially developed at realization level; no exact general geometric-level theorem found in the recorded searches.',['Deligne1974HodgeIII','Bondarko2018Dimension'],['S03','S10'],'No counterexample claimed; dimension/level conventions must be fixed before proving a stronger bound.','medium')
c(3014,[3013],[(108,'258-272'),(114,'430-443')],'Deligne ... Leray',
 'For a smooth projective morphism with a relative hard-Lefschetz operator, its derived cohomology complex splits as the direct sum of its cohomology sheaves, yielding the relevant Leray degeneration for composition.',
 'stated without proof','B','Deligne1968 Theorem1.5 and §2.4 give the splitting criterion and smooth-projective application. His §2.6.4 still distinguishes the l-adic Lefschetz input then known in characteristic0 from the conjectural general case; later Weil results supply it.','Received1July1968; published28December1968.','Established with hard Lefschetz. This does not justify replacing an arbitrary admissible sheaf by a direct summand without an additional argument.',['Deligne1968Lefschetz','Deligne1980WeilII'],['S04','S06','S09'])
c(3015,[3019],[(108,'258-280')],'F facteur direct de R^j g_*Q_l',
 'If X is smooth separated of dimension n and F is a direct summand of R^j g_*Q_l for g smooth projective of relative dimension j, total-space splitting gives ordinary weights [i+j,2(n+j)] and the stated coarser refinements; geometric levels retain their motivic assumptions.',
 'proof sketched','C','Deligne1968 splitting and Weil II 3.3 establish these realization estimates by a direct-summand argument. The stricter direct-summand hypothesis on scan113 is genuinely stronger than general subquotient admissibility.','1968/1980.','The coarse bounds have a valid route with the precise coefficient realization; devissage to all source-admissible coefficients is not automatic.',['Deligne1968Lefschetz','Deligne1980WeilII'],['S06','S08','S09'])
c(3016,[3016],[(108,'279-280'),(120,'803')],'remplacer2j parj?',
 'For smooth curves and a smooth-projective weight-one coefficient F, the proposed ordinary upper bound2i+j fails. The Legendre family over U=P¹_F5 minus{0,1,∞}, F=R¹g_*Q_l, has a weight4 quotient of H¹(Ubar,F), whereas i=j=n=1 gives bound3.',
 'open on the pages','B','Weil II 1.8.4 gives the local monodromy mechanism; 3.5.5 gives SL₂ geometric monodromy for the nonconstant Legendre family. At0 and1 split multiplicative reduction gives R¹j_*F=Q_l(−2); H²(P¹bar,j_*F)=0, so these local terms are quotients of H¹.','Local weight theorem published1980; explicit application checked here.','The proposed sharpening is false even for the exact direct-summand coefficient family used in the manuscript.',['Deligne1980WeilII','Ito2020WeightMonodromy'],['S08','S13','S14'],'programs/check_calculations.py: Legendre invariants and local Tate matrix; proofs.md §4')
c(3017,[3015],[(108,'284')],'poids≥j?',
 'Under the same Legendre hypotheses, H_c¹(Ubar,F) contains the weight0 boundary invariant space from0 and1. Thus the proposed compact-support lower boundj=1 is false.',
 'open on the pages','B','The j_!→j_* boundary exact sequence and H⁰(Ubar,F)=0 produce the injection. Weil II 3.5.5 checks the needed lack of global invariants; local split multiplicative degeneration checks the weight0 invariants.','Weil II1980; explicit application checked here.','False with every geometric/coefficient hypothesis retained. This is separate from compact upper weights≤i+j.',['Deligne1980WeilII','Ito2020WeightMonodromy'],['S08','S13','S14'],'programs/check_calculations.py: Legendre family; proofs.md §4')
c(3018,[3014],[(109,'289-304')],'poids ...≤Inf(2i,2n)',
 'A bound omitting the coefficient degree j fails already on X=Spec k with F=H¹(E,Q_l) for an elliptic curve: i=n=0 but F has weight and Hodge level1. The preceding coefficient formulas have i+j.',
 'open on the pages','F','The point example and Deligne1968 splitting isolate the missing coefficient shift. The manuscript marks the passage with a question and the scan must decide whether j was omitted by the writer or the transcription.','1968/1974 supporting results.','Literal formula false; plausible correction requires adding j. No source text is changed.',['Deligne1968Lefschetz','Deligne1974HodgeIII'],['S06','S03'],'programs/check_calculations.py: point/elliptic endpoint; proofs.md §3')
c(3019,[3007,3018],[(111,'318-348')],'Lemme2.2; Corollaire2.3',
 'With special sheaves defined as subquotients of appropriate smooth-projective cohomology: subquotients/sums preserve weight class, tensor products add weights, pullback preserves the construction, and duality changes the class by the displayed Tate twist. Padding by projective spaces handles the prescribed relative dimension.',
 'proof sketched','B','The formal subquotient, Künneth and duality arguments are compatible with Deligne1968 smooth-projective splitting and Weil II purity. These are closure claims about the defined geometric class, not a characterization of all pure lisse sheaves.','Framework1968/1980.','The formal closures survive; the separate weight0 iff statement is isolated as C3062.',['Deligne1968Lefschetz','Deligne1980WeilII','Demazure1971Motifs'],['S06','S08','S22','S23'],'Independent proof in proofs.md §9; no numerical computation.')
c(3020,[3017,3018],[(111,'350-356'),(112,'364-386')],'Proposition3.3; stratification',
 'The effective/admissible classes obtained by finite filtrations and finite stratifications are stable under the claimed subquotients, extensions and pullback. The dual weight set must be2n−Φ with elements2n−i, not n−i.',
 'proof sketched','C','Filtration refinement proves the formal closure statements independently. Weil II supplies numerical mixed-weight filtrations. No equivalence of the source geometric class with every modern mixed lisse sheaf is asserted; universal-homeomorphism invariance is a separate étale fact.','Weil II1980; formal constructions standard.','Formal stability is established under the source definition; the scan112 index remains a reading alarm.',['Deligne1980WeilII','Deligne1974HodgeIII'],['S03','S08','S24'],'proofs.md §9; dual-weight arithmetic in calculations.json.')
c(3021,[3018],[(112,'386-390'),(113,'396-402')],'Proposition4.2',
 'Joint weight-and-level admissibility means one filtration whose pieces satisfy both constraints. It is stronger than separately admitting a weight filtration and a level filtration, and its formal closure operations must use common refinements.',
 'proof sketched','C','Filtration refinement proves the formal operations where the class is Serre-stable. Hodge III and Weil II supply realization filtrations, but do not identify the manuscript geometric level with Hodge level or show all independently specified filtrations coincide.','1974/1980.','Formal joint closure is supported; equality with separate constraints remains distinct and open in the source.',['Deligne1974HodgeIII','Deligne1980WeilII'],['S03','S08'],'proofs.md §9')
c(3022,[3018],[(111,'354-356'),(112,'390')],'intersection?; plus forte',
 'For source-admissible geometric sheaves, determine whether membership in two weight sets forces membership in their intersection, and whether separately available weight and level constraints admit a joint filtration.',
 'open on the pages','C','Weil II3.4.1 gives the unique numerical weight filtration for mixed lisse sheaves over finite fields. It answers the numerical weight-intersection question there, but not the stronger geometric-generation/joint-level assertion. Grothendieck1969 explains why effective Hodge type cannot simply stand for geometric support.',
 '1974/1980 realization formalism; Grothendieck paper received1968/published1969.','Numerical subproblem settled; exact geometric joint-filtration problem not settled by the searched references.',['Deligne1980WeilII','Grothendieck1969HodgeFalse'],['S08','S17','S23'],'No numerical computation.','medium')
c(3023,[3019],[(113,'406-424'),(114,'430-447')],'Théorème5.1',
 'For f:X→S of relative dimension≤n and F a direct summand of R^j g_*Q_l for g smooth projective of the specified relative dimension, the stated R^i f_! upper weight i+j and lower range2(i−n) in the relevant degrees follow through total-space splitting; the level conclusions use the manuscript motivic assumptions.',
 'proof sketched','C','Deligne1968§2.4 supplies the summand step, Weil II3.3.1 and3.3.3–5 the numerical direct-image estimates. Strong resolution, semisimplicity and geometric-level generation in the written proof are not all consequences of these numerical results.','1968/1980; alteration replacement1996.','Realization estimates established under the exact coefficient hypotheses; full categorical formulation remains conditional.',['Deligne1968Lefschetz','Deligne1980WeilII','deJong1996Alterations'],['S06','S08','S23','S24'])
c(3024,[3019],[(114,'451-453')],'Corollaire5.2',
 'The stated extension of Theorem5.1 to the broader source-admissible class is conditional on the Tate/semisimplicity and devissage inputs explicitly retained on the page.',
 'stated without proof (conditional)','C','Weil II establishes direct-image bounds for numerical mixed sheaves, and de Jong4.1 supplies alterations rather than the exact birational resolution. Neither proves all the algebraic-correspondence/level assumptions of this corollary.','1980/1996.','Do not report the categorical corollary as unconditional merely because the weight theorem is known.',['Deligne1980WeilII','deJong1996Alterations'],['S08','S24'])
c(3025,[3020],[(114,'457')],'sans [Pro] / sous [Codex] conj. type Tate',
 'The corollary claims closure of effective admissibility under R^i f_!, either without Tate-type conjectures (Pro) or under Tate-type conjectures (Codex): the surviving preposition differs between the two supplied transcriptions.',
 'stated without proof (reading disputed)','F','Weil II proves numerical mixedness under direct images; de Jong gives a later alteration route for many reductions. Neither statement alone proves closure of the source geometric admissibility class without the semisimplicity/devissage input, and this history cannot decide the ink.','1980/1996.','Exact original hypothesis unresolved; retain both readings and do not attribute an unconditional geometric admissibility theorem to the author on the Pro wording alone.',['Deligne1980WeilII','deJong1996Alterations'],['S08','S24'],'No computation; readings.tsv records decisive variant.','high')
c(3026,[3021],[(115,'461-467')],'2(n−i+j)',
 'The displayed ordinary level bound2(n−i+j) is false for a smooth proper elliptic curve with constant coefficients: n=i=1,j=0 gives0, while H¹ has Hodge level1. The surrounding formula2n−i+j gives1.',
 'false as stated (displayed formula)','F','Hodge III confirms the elliptic Hodge-type obstruction; the preceding direct-image and duality formulas support2n−i+j as a proposed reading. The source itself asks to verify the yoga.','Hodge III1974.','Scan correction candidate only; no silently corrected theorem.',['Deligne1974HodgeIII'],['S03'],'programs/check_calculations.py: endpoint arithmetic; proofs.md §3')
c(3027,[3022],[(116,'504-518')],'R^qg_* ... puissance extérieure',
 'For the complement g of a simple normal crossings divisor in a regular scheme with l invertible, R¹g_*Q_l is the sum of divisor-supported Q_l(−1), and R^qg_*Q_l is its qth exterior power, with the usual intersection supports.',
 'proof sketched','B','Riou2010, Propositions2.2–2.3, states the smooth-divisor and SNC formulas using Gabber absolute purity, Theorem2.1. This checks the missing/extra Tate-twist alarms on scan116.','Riou notes dated7December2010; underlying purity theorem earlier, exact first circulation not established here.','Established with regularity, SNC and invertibility.',['Riou2010Purity'],['S15'],'Twist bookkeeping checked in calculations.json; proofs.md §5.')
c(3028,[3022],[(116,'523-540'),(118,'574-693'),(120,'784-795')],'0≤i≤2n−1; niveau≤min(i,2n−1−i)',
 'For a nontrivial boundary point of an immersion of a smooth n-dimensional variety, n≥1, under the stated SNC resolution setup, local ordinary cohomology has the displayed numerical weight and Hodge-level bounds. The initial uniform2n−2 bound is sharpened to n−1 on scan120.',
 'corrected by himself later (116 to120)','C','Riou2.3 gives the SNC sheaves; proper-support Hodge-type bounds from Hodge III yield the local Hodge box reproduced in proofs.md. Durfee–Saito1990 Theorem4.1 is a related link-weight theorem under different intersection-cohomology hypotheses, not an automatic proof of every source geometric-level assertion.','Hodge III1974; Durfee–Saito1990; Riou2010.','Numerical/Hodge bounds follow in the explicit resolution setting; motivic level remains conditional. The n=0/nonboundary case must be excluded from a2n−1 cutoff.',['Riou2010Purity','Deligne1974HodgeIII','DurfeeSaito1990'],['S03','S06','S08','S15'],'programs/check_calculations.py: local Hodge-box enumeration n=1..8 plus general proof in proofs.md §5.')
c(3029,[3023],[(120,'771-776')],'s’identifie avec [Pro] / intervient avec [Codex]',
 'Identifying the highest local weight piece with the entire indicated E2 term is false; saying the E2 term contributes can be correct. For the punctured strict local regular surface, blow up the closed point then a smooth point of the exceptional divisor: E2^{0,2}≠0 but its d2 is injective and H² of the puncture vanishes.',
 'false as stated (strong identification reading)','F','Riou absolute purity computes H²=0 and the SNC formula supplies the nonzero E2 term. The two exceptional P¹ intersection matrix has determinant1. This is a precise Leray counterexample, not a denial of the separate weight-spectral-sequence degeneration C3009.','Purity/SNC formulation checked in Riou2010; example independently reconstructed.','The Codex weaker wording must remain a live alternative until scan inspection.',['Riou2010Purity','Deligne1974HodgeIII'],['S15','S03'],'programs/check_calculations.py: double-blowup intersection matrix and d2 ranks; proofs.md §6')
c(3030,[3016],[(120,'800-804')],'poids≤2min(i,n)+j?',
 'For the punctured local curve at a split multiplicative Legendre degeneration, F=R¹ of its smooth projective elliptic family satisfies the source coefficient hypothesis, but H¹(I,F)=Q_l(−2) has weight4, exceeding2min(1,1)+1=3.',
 'open on the pages','B','Weil II1.8.4 and Ito Remark3.2.11 describe the unipotent Tate degeneration; the explicit inertia cohomology is V_I(−1). Infinite unipotent tame monodromy must not be replaced by finite monodromy.','Weil II1980; Ito first2017, published2020.','Proposed numerical upper bound false. The separate geometric-level refinement on the next line is not refuted by this weight calculation.',['Deligne1980WeilII','Ito2020WeightMonodromy'],['S08','S13','S14'],'programs/check_calculations.py: Tate matrix and coinvariant twist; proofs.md §4')
c(3031,[3024],[(120,'823-836')],'d_y(f); peut-on remplacer?',
 'The local dimension invariant is defined using the closure of the image near y; replacing it by actual fibre dimension in the proposed local estimate fails for an open immersion, whose fibres have dimension0 while boundary cohomology is nonzero.',
 'open on the pages','C','The smooth divisor formula Riou2.2 gives R¹j_*Q_l=Q_l(−1) at a missing point, providing a direct obstruction to the fibre-dimension substitution. The full refined invariant conjecture is not identified with a published theorem by the recorded searches.','Divisor formula checked in Riou2010.','Naive fibre-dimension replacement false; source d_y-based formulation remains a separate, partly specified problem.',['Riou2010Purity','DurfeeSaito1990'],['S06','S15'],'Use j:G_m→A¹, n=1, nonzero R¹j at0; proofs.md §5.','medium')
c(3032,[3016,3022],[(122,'838-890')],'à voir; dimension ...?',
 'The coefficient argument rewrites the stalk cohomology via a smooth projective family of relative dimension j, but the needed bounds for the q-indexed boundary strata and resulting E2 terms are left unfinished.',
 'open on the pages','C','Deligne1968 splitting and Riou SNC purity justify individual steps. The Legendre counterexample rules out the overly sharp weight target; no opened theorem with exactly the remaining geometric-level and d_y quantifiers was found.','1968/1980/2010 ingredients.','The coarse direct-summand method is usable; the exact unfinished refinement remains unresolved in the searched literature and should be reformulated before research.',['Deligne1968Lefschetz','Riou2010Purity','Deligne1980WeilII'],['S06','S08','S15'],'Calculation certificate checks the false target, not a proof of every unfinished inequality.','medium')
c(3033,[3025],[(125,'897-934')],'Lemme1',
 'For the late, untwisted dimension-type filtration generated by cohomology of smooth projective fibres of dimension≤d and closed under finite filtrations/stratifications, subquotients and extensions preserve type≤d.',
 'proved there','E','This is the formal Serre-envelope closure property of the displayed definition. Modern dimension filtrations such as Bondarko2018 use a different triangulated category and are comparison sources, not proof that all conventions coincide.','Formal property standard; modern comparison first2016/published2018.','Established by the definition and filtration refinement. It is distinct from the earlier Tate-stable D_d convention on scans35–36.',['Bondarko2018Dimension','Demazure1971Motifs'],['S10','S14','S23'],'Independent filtration proof in proofs.md §9.')
c(3034,[3025],[(125,'936')],'Lemme2',
 'Pullback preserves the late dimension-type bound, since a smooth projective generating family stays smooth projective of the same relative dimension after base change, and pullback of field-valued étale sheaves is exact.',
 'proved there','E','This is formal base change of the defining generators and exactness of inverse image. It is not the doubtful pullback of the integral smooth-motive category on scan142.','Formal sheaf theory at the time; comparison definitions checked in Demazure1971.','Valid for the displayed sheaf-type filtration.',['Demazure1971Motifs','Riou2010Purity'],['S15','S23'],'proofs.md §9')
c(3035,[3025,3027],[(127,'938-970'),(129,'987-989')],'Lemme3 ...d+d′',
 'The proposed compactly supported direct-image type bound is d+d′ for a morphism of fibre dimension≤d′. The proof uses stratification, a radicial reduction, compactification and the generating smooth-projective family; complete reducibility is explicitly questioned.',
 'proof sketched','C','Deligne1968 and de Jong4.1 support realization/splitting and alteration reductions. They do not by themselves establish the exact untwisted geometric-type statement over every source base, nor supply an unstated semisimplicity input.','1968/1996.','A conditional source argument remains; no exact unconditional theorem with all stated quantifiers was identified in recorded searches.',['Deligne1968Lefschetz','deJong1996Alterations','Bondarko2018Dimension'],['S06','S10','S24'],'No stronger proof event is assigned than targeted argument check.','medium')
c(3036,[3026],[(133,'1039-1052'),(137,'1152-1155')],'type≤d+d′−1',
 'The strict improvement d+d′−1 fails for the late untwisted type convention. For j:G_m^r→A^r with constant coefficients, the top local cohomology is Q_l(−r), weight2r, and cannot be generated by untwisted cohomology of varieties of dimension≤r−1.',
 'false as stated','B','Riou2.3 computes the exterior top local term. Weil purity bounds the weights of every untwisted generating cohomology by2(r−1), and subquotients/extensions preserve that range. Tate-stabilizing the type would change the definition and cannot repair this exact claim.','Purity1980; SNC formulation2010.','The bound without−1 is not refuted. The author also writes a weaker d result on scan137; preserve both stages.',['Riou2010Purity','Deligne1980WeilII'],['S08','S15'],'programs/check_calculations.py: SNC top Tate type obstruction; proofs.md §7')
c(3037,[3027],[(131,'999-1031'),(135,'1078-1119')],'ramener ...d′=1; à vérifier',
 'The attempted reduction to relative dimension1 uses Artin-style affine curves, localization sequences, and factoring a smooth projective coefficient into a proper family; the final required implication is explicitly marked unverified.',
 'proof sketched','C','Deligne1968 splitting and de Jong’s curve-fibration/alteration proof show later usable ingredients, but do not retroactively fill the written implication for the exact type filtration. The source base-change/localization indices also have transcription alarms.','1968/1996.','The reduction is a legitimate research lead, not a completed proof. It must target a corrected bound after C3036.',['Deligne1968Lefschetz','deJong1996Alterations'],['S06','S24'],'The affine-curve two-row sequence is checked formally in proofs.md §8.','medium')
c(3038,[3027],[(137,'1120-1168')],'trois couches; E₃',
 'For a spectral sequence with only rows q=0,1,2, the middle row has no incoming or outgoing differentials after d2; hence E3^{p,1}=E∞^{p,1}. The abutment has three filtration layers, while the outer rows can still be linked by d3.',
 'proof sketched','E','This follows directly from the bidegree d_r:(p,q)→(p+r,q−r+1). Deligne1968 provides the relevant Leray context; no global E3 degeneration is inferred.','Formal spectral-sequence fact; context published1968.','The middle-row stabilization is valid; interpreting it as stabilization of all rows would overstate the source.',['Deligne1968Lefschetz'],['S06','S09'],'Independent index proof in proofs.md §8.')
c(3039,[3028],[(125,'913-918')],'il revient au même ...fibres géométriques',
 'A criterion based only on individual geometric stalks loses arithmetic descent and monodromy. For instance a nontrivial infinite unipotent arithmetic self-extension on SpecF_q has the same underlying geometric vector space as the constant rank2 sheaf.',
 'stated without proof','C','Weil II3.4.1 explicitly distinguishes arithmetic sheaves from geometric semisimplicity. Fibrewise geometric generation does not alone produce the source-relative geometric family and descent datum. Additional hypotheses may repair the criterion.','1980.','The naive equivalence is unsupported; the counterexample demonstrates information loss, not a fully specified counterexample to every possible interpretation of type.',['Deligne1980WeilII'],['S08','S09'],'programs/check_calculations.py: unipotent representation; proofs.md §2','medium')
c(3040,[3034],[(140,'1182-1187')],'je conjecture ...un seul ...deux',
 'For semisimple rational motives over K=Frac(S), S normal integral, is unramifiedness of realizations for one everywhere-invertible prime, or for two distinct primes on their invertibility loci, sufficient for all-prime smoothness?',
 'open on the pages','C','The Néron–Ogg–Shafarevich theorem supplies the one-prime criterion for H¹ of abelian varieties over suitable traits. Serre–Tate1968 is the exact primary reference. The broad motive/base assertion was searched in arXiv, MathOverflow, zbMATH and SGA-related sources; no exact general theorem was located. Compatible Frobenius polynomials alone do not prove local unramifiedness.','NOS communicated earlier; Serre–Tate published1968. Exact first date not reconstructed here.','Settled abelian/trait case; general source assertion not identified as settled.',['SerreTate1968GoodReduction','VasiuZink2010'],['S11','S21','S24'],'No counterexample to the all-prime independence assertion is claimed.','medium')
c(3041,[3035],[(140,'1189-1206'),(140,'1228-1230')],'Hom ...Z-module de type fini',
 'Finite generation of integral Hom follows only with a common almost-all-prime reference system and rational finite-dimensional Hom. Under a weaker arbitrary-lattice reading, target lattices l⁻¹Z_l for every prime and source lattices Z_l give a Hom subgroup ofQ containing1/l for every prime, which is not finitely generated.',
 'stated without proof (definition-dependent)','F','Tate1966 first-page integral/rational comparison and Faltings1986 illustrate coherent integral realization conditions. They do not define the manuscript’s missing reference system. The weak-axiom counterexample is independently proved; the correctly normalized definition is left explicit.','Tate1966; Faltings1983/1986.','The literal weakened interpretation fails. The exact intended almost-all-prime condition cannot be recovered from the excerpt alone.',['Tate1966Endomorphisms','Faltings1986Finiteness'],['S14','S15','S20'],'programs/check_calculations.py: lattice-denominator family; proofs.md §10')
c(3042,[3036],[(140,'1228-1234')],'pleinement fidèle; abélienne',
 'Restriction from a normal integral S to its generic point is fully faithful for unramified realization systems, because G_K→π₁(S) is surjective. Kernels/cokernels are formed componentwise once the assumed rational motive category and exact realizations exist.',
 'proof sketched','C','The normal-scheme connected-cover argument proves the generic full-faithfulness independently; SGA1 and étale purity give the published context. Abelianness remains conditional on the source’s rational category and well-defined common lattices.','SGA1 seminar1960–61, printed1971; exact rational motive assumptions remain conjectural.','Valid conditional construction, not an unconditional category of all standard-conjectural motives.',['SGA1','Faltings1986Finiteness'],['S15','S24'],'Independent categorical proof in proofs.md §10.')
c(3043,[3037],[(140,'1234-1237')],'épaisse',
 'If épaisse means extension-closed inside the generic integral category, it is false. On S=SpecC[[t]], a ramified tame cyclic-l cover gives a nontrivial unipotent F_l² representation extending two constant F_l representations. Its rational motive is0, so the torsion example satisfies the generic integral setup.',
 'false as stated (extension-closed interpretation)','B','The finite-étale interpretation in SGA1 and the explicit representation distinguish subquotient closure from extension closure. There is no contradiction with full faithfulness or internal abelianness. The historical meaning of épaisse is a textual qualification.','SGA1 printed1971; elementary counterexample checked here.','The weaker full subcategory stable under subobjects/quotients survives.',['SGA1','DeligneMilne1982Tannakian'],['S24','S22'],'programs/check_calculations.py: finite unipotent matrix; proofs.md §10')
c(3044,[3038],[(140,'1237'),(141,'1242-1245')],'catégorie quotient ...équivalente',
 'Rationalization has the finite torsion systems as its kernel. If every rational object has a coherent integral lattice and rational morphisms can be cleared by one integer, the internal Serre quotient of integral objects by finite objects is equivalent to the rational category.',
 'proof sketched','C','This is a conditional localization argument, independently checked. Tate1966’s integral Hom criterion illustrates the denominator condition in the abelian finite-field case; it does not construct every motivic reference lattice.','Tate1966; formal Serre localization predates these pages.','Correct with the explicitly stated categorical/lattice assumptions; ambient extension closure is unnecessary.',['Tate1966Endomorphisms','DeligneMilne1982Tannakian'],['S20','S22'],'proofs.md §10')
c(3045,[3039],[(141,'1247-1250')],'⊗-catégorie avecHom',
 'The intended integral realization systems admit componentwise tensor product and internal Hom. Finite torsion objects are not dualizable: Hom_Z(Z/l,Z)=0, so the displayed tensor claim is not a claim of rigid Tannakian structure.',
 'stated without proof','C','Deligne–Milne1982§1 distinguishes internal Hom from rigidity. The elementary torsion calculation verifies the limitation; closure of all componentwise constructions still requires the missing coherent-lattice axiom.','1982 reference; algebra elementary earlier.','Tensor/internal-Hom construction is conditional; nonrigidity is an inference of the notes/current run, not an author claim.',['DeligneMilne1982Tannakian'],['S22'],'programs/check_calculations.py: torsion duality obstruction; proofs.md §10')
c(3046,[3040],[(141,'1257-1259')],'Tout schéma propre et lisse',
 'Every proper smooth X→S is asserted to define the integral cohomological objects, including their relative étale-site versions. Proper smooth base change provides lisse l-adic systems; rational motivic existence and a uniform integral reference remain additional assumptions.',
 'stated without proof','C','SGA proper smooth base change and Weil II3.3.9 support the realizations. The claim is proper smooth, not merely projective. None of the opened modern Nori/André comparisons supplies the exact standard-conjectural integral category on arbitrary S.','SGA4 printed1972–73; Weil II1980.','Established realization ingredient; conditional full motivic object assertion.',['Deligne1980WeilII','SGA4','Arapura2013MotivicSheaves'],['S08','S17','S18','S24'])
c(3047,[3041],[(141,'1261-1285')],'E_l=Hom(...,Q_l/Z_l)',
 'For an extension0→A⁰→A→F→0 with abelian A⁰ and finite component group F, the l-primary torsion sequence dualizes to0→Hom(F(l),Q_l/Z_l)→E_l→H¹(A⁰,Z_l)→0 under the displayed realization convention.',
 'proof sketched','E','Divisibility of the geometric abelian points and injectivity of Q_l/Z_l make the dual sequence exact. Tate1966 checks the relevant l-adic dual convention. The margin’s prose may reverse extension order; the definition itself need not be wrong.','Tate1966; underlying torsion algebra standard.','Finite component contribution is the kernel in this contravariant realization.',['Tate1966Endomorphisms'],['S20'],'Independent exact-sequence proof in proofs.md §10; no numerical computation.')
c(3048,[3042],[(142,'1295-1296')],'on se ramène ...SpecK',
 'The weight-one contravariant functor is asserted fully faithful. For abelian schemes, generic rational h¹ morphisms and all integral lattice conditions recover generic homomorphisms; already-existing generic homomorphisms extend over a normal base. Disconnected component extensions require their own argument.',
 'proof sketched','C','Faltings1986§2Lemma1 proves extension of generic homomorphisms between already-existing semiabelian schemes over a normal base. Tate1966 proves finite-field integral full faithfulness for one prime. These are ingredients, not a proof that all disconnected-group systems lie in the essential image.','Tate1966; Faltings first1983, English1986.','Abelian-scheme morphism ingredient established; exact entire source functor remains conditional on rational motives and component data.',['Faltings1986Finiteness','Tate1966Endomorphisms'],['S14','S15','S20'],'proofs.md §10')
c(3049,[3043],[(142,'1298-1317')],'La question si ...suffisante; peut-être pas ...dim2',
 'The source retracts emphatic sufficiency and asks whether weight-one effectivity plus all-prime nonramification implies an abelian-scheme realization over a regular base. It does not in mixed-characteristic dimension2.',
 'corrected by himself later (deleted certainty, then open question)','B','Vasiu–Zink2010Theorem28 gives regular local rings R=W(k)[[x,y]]/(p−(xy)^{p−1}) whose punctured spectra carry nonextendable abelian schemes. For l≠p purity extends all l^m covers; for l=p the required S[1/p] is already inside the puncture. Corollary4 gives a positive low-ramification case p∉m^p. Their references trace the obstruction to earlier Raynaud–Gabber work.','Vasiu–Zink first4Sep2009, revised1July2010, published2010; obstruction earlier, no claim of first discovery2010.','Exact all-prime sufficiency fails; low-ramification extension and NOS over traits remain positive cases.',['VasiuZink2010','Stacks0BMA','SerreTate1968GoodReduction'],['S01','S02','S03','S06','S15','S21'],'programs/check_calculations.py: ring dimension/ramification; proofs.md §11 checks every hypothesis.')
c(3050,[3044],[(142,'1313-1317')],'indice premier [Pro] / ordre premier [Codex]',
 'A possible reduction of the finite-flat-group extension obstruction to prime index or prime order is suggested, but the two transcriptions do not agree on the quantifier.',
 'open on the pages','F','Vasiu–ZinkLemma27 turns a finite-flat morphism that is epimorphic only off the closed point into a nonextendable abelian scheme. It does not, from the viewed statement, establish the exact prime-index/order reduction written here.','Lemma27 published2010; source wording unresolved.','Keep as a reading-dependent open step; no unnamed criterion or prime-order theorem is manufactured.',['VasiuZink2010'],['S02','S03'],'No computation establishes the reduction.','medium')
c(3051,[3004],[(142,'1319-1327')],'difficulté ...K→K′',
 'The source-defined integral smooth-motive category is generically based on K. Pullback along a map S′→S whose image misses the generic point lacks a canonical field map K→K′ and needs a genuinely relative construction.',
 'open on the pages','C','Arapura2013 constructs relative motivic sheaves over smooth characteristic-zero bases and compares pure objects with André motives (Theorem6.4.1). Kahn2025 handles finite Galois descent. Neither alone defines arbitrary base-change functors for the exact integral category in these pages.','Arapura first2008/published2013; Kahn first2023/published2025.','The gap is real in the displayed definition; later relative theories provide comparison directions under different coefficients and hypotheses.',['Arapura2013MotivicSheaves','Kahn2025Descent'],['S17','S18','S19','S23'],'No missing generic field map is silently supplied.')
c(3052,[3045],[(144,'1346-1382'),(146,'1451-1472')],'équivalence; au grain de sel',
 'For additive C and idempotent-complete additive D, restriction gives an equivalence Fun_add(Kar(C),D)→Fun_add(C,D), including natural transformations. The extension is not a literal unique functor-object assignment independent of choices.',
 'proof sketched','B','Demazure1971§3Proposition1(iii) publishes the additive universal property of the motivic projector completion and cites SGA4IV7.5. The complete categorical equivalence is independently proved by image projectors and extension of natural transformations.','Bourbaki talk1969–70, printed1971; SGA4 printed1972.','Valid with additivity and idempotent completeness; variance/deletion on scan144 remains a scan alarm.',['Demazure1971Motifs','SGA4'],['S22','S23'],'Independent proof in proofs.md §12.')
c(3053,[3046],[(145,'1391-1400')],'Hom ...[deleted]',
 'In an additive F-linear category, finite-dimensional End(X) for every object implies finite-dimensional Hom(X,Y), since Hom(X,Y) is a corner of End(X⊕Y). Without biproducts the implication fails.',
 'proof sketched','E','Jannsen1992Lemma2 uses the additive pseudo-abelian finite-End criterion. The corner embedding gives the independent proof and explains the source deletion of a redundant Hom hypothesis.','Underlying algebra standard; Jannsen application1992.','Valid in the additive setting actually required by the construction.',['Jannsen1992Motives','Demazure1971Motifs'],['S01','S02','S23'],'programs/check_calculations.py: missing-additivity triangular example; proofs.md §12.')
c(3054,[3047],[(145,'1403-1409')],'ap=p′a ...quotient',
 'The quotient of intertwiners ap=p′a by those with ap=p′a=0 identifies with p′Hom(X,Y)p via a↦p′ap. This is the usual morphism space in Kar(C).',
 'proved there','E','Demazure1971§3 gives the same corner construction. The quotient identification is independently verified algebraically: any intertwiner differs from its corner by a null intertwiner, and every corner is fixed by the map.','Published comparison1971; elementary algebra earlier.','Correct; the quotient should not be omitted from the manuscript description.',['Demazure1971Motifs'],['S22','S23'],'programs/check_calculations.py: explicit corner dimension check; proofs.md §12.')
c(3055,[3047],[(146,'1427-1472')],'tout projecteur ...noyau',
 'The inclusion C→Kar(C) is fully faithful, and every projector e on(X,p) splits as the object(X,e) with complementary summand(X,p−e). No semisimplicity is needed.',
 'proved there','B','Demazure1971§3Proposition1(i)–(ii) states precisely these properties; they are independently checked from the projector identities.','Talk1969–70; published1971.','Established formal completion theorem.',['Demazure1971Motifs'],['S22','S23'],'Independent projector proof in proofs.md §12.')
c(3056,[3048,3050],[(146,'1466-1468'),(147,'1478-1549')],'abélienne semi-simple',
 'An F-linear additive idempotent-complete category whose endomorphism algebra of every object is finite-dimensional semisimple is semisimple abelian.',
 'proof sketched','B','Jannsen1992Lemma2 states the exact criterion. The independent proof uses Artin–Wedderburn decomposition of End(X⊕Y), constructs generalized inverses in matrix corners, and then kernels/cokernels; it does not assume abelianness at the circular-sounding source phrase.','Jannsen received12July1991, published1992.','Established abstract theorem. Applying it to homological motives still needs the missing semisimplicity hypothesis; numerical motives satisfy JannsenTheorem1.',['Jannsen1992Motives','Demazure1971Motifs'],['S01','S02','S23'],'General proof in proofs.md §12 plus exact rectangular certificate.')
c(3057,[3048],[(147,'1498-1530')],'uvu=u; vuv=v',
 'Every rectangular morphism u in a semisimple additive setting admits v with uvu=u and vuv=v; vu and uv are the source/target projectors of its isomorphism block.',
 'proved there','E','The Artin–Wedderburn block argument proves the assertion; JannsenLemma2 provides the exact categorical setting. The displayed source identities are independently checked on a non-square rational example, supplementing rather than replacing the general proof.','Algebra standard before these pages; later explicit categorical application1992.','Correct with the semisimple endomorphism-algebra hypothesis.',['Jannsen1992Motives'],['S01','S02'],'programs/check_calculations.py: rectangular generalized inverse; proofs.md §12.')
c(3058,[3049],[(148,'1565-1583')],'Tout morphisme; v=inj₂u pr₂',
 'Every morphism decomposes as A⊕B→B⊕C, zero on A and an isomorphism on B. With this order of summands, the inverse-block construction uses pr₁ on B⊕C; the displayed pr₂ is ill-typed.',
 'proof sketched (displayed projection index disputed)','B','The semisimple-category criterion in JannsenLemma2 and the independent block proof establish the normal form. Both supplied transcriptions say every morphism; a prior paraphrase restricted to monomorphisms overstates a source limitation.','Jannsen1992 comparison; formal algebra earlier.','Normal form valid; projection index requires scan verification and is not silently edited.',['Jannsen1992Motives'],['S01','S02'],'programs/check_calculations.py: normal form/generalized inverse; proofs.md §12.')
c(3059,[3050],[(147,'1539-1549')],'noethérien et artinien',
 'Each object in the above semisimple category has finite length, hence ascending and descending chains of subobjects terminate. A finite-dimensional endomorphism algebra cannot contain arbitrarily many nonzero mutually orthogonal idempotents.',
 'proof sketched','E','JannsenLemma2’s semisimple abelian conclusion and the finite Artin–Wedderburn decomposition prove finite length. Finite-dimensionality of End alone without semisimplicity/additivity is not the claim.','Underlying algebra standard; comparison1992.','Valid after the categorical kernels and splitting have been constructed.',['Jannsen1992Motives'],['S01','S02'],'Independent finite-idempotent proof in proofs.md §12.')
c(3060,[3051],[(149,'1598-1616')],'bifoncteur biadditif',
 'A biadditive functor C×D→E extends to Kar(C)×Kar(D)→Kar(E) by (X,p),(Y,q)↦(F(X,Y),F(p,q)). Symmetric monoidal constraints extend functorially and retain their coherence.',
 'proof sketched','B','Demazure1971§3 explicitly defines the motivic tensor product by tensoring projectors. The universal projector construction proves extension of constraints; no duals or fibre functor follow without further hypotheses.','Talk1969–70; published1971.','Established formal tensor extension.',['Demazure1971Motifs','DeligneMilne1982Tannakian'],['S22','S23'],'Independent coherence proof in proofs.md §12.')
c(3061,[3052],[(149,'1616-1625')],'caractéristique0; puissances symétriques et extérieures',
 'In a Q-linear idempotent-complete symmetric monoidal category, averaging and signed-averaging idempotents in Q[S_r] split to define Sym^r and wedge^r; more general rational Schur projectors split as well.',
 'proof sketched','E','The group-algebra identities and pseudo-abelian property suffice. Deligne–Milne§1 gives the tensor-category setting and Demazure§3 the projector completion; no identical integral assertion is made.','Formal characteristic-zero construction standard; cited expositions1971/1982.','Correct with the denominators invertible; source characteristic0 is essential.',['Demazure1971Motifs','DeligneMilne1982Tannakian'],['S22','S23'],'programs/check_calculations.py: S3 symmetric/alternating projectors; proofs.md §12.')
c(3062,[3066],[(111,'318-322')],'si i=0 ...F localement constant',
 'Under the displayed Q_l-subquotient definition, special weight0 sheaves have finite monodromy: R⁰ of a smooth projective morphism is a finite permutation representation on connected components. Conversely any finite-monodromy Q_l-sheaf is a summand of a finite étale permutation generator. Arbitrary lisse sheaves need not have finite monodromy.',
 'false as stated (if locally constant means arbitrary lisse Q_l-sheaf)','B','The finite-cover description in SGA1 and semisimple finite-group representation theory establish the corrected equivalence. A Frobenius-unipotent infinite representation on SpecF_q is lisse but not special weight0. CodexK12-07 has a deleted converse warning; the separate Q-versus-Q_l objection does not defeat the finite regular-representation argument.','SGA1 printed1971; finite-group Maschke theory earlier.','Corrected statement: special weight0 iff finite monodromy. A historical locally-constant convention may make the surviving source phrase correct, so ink/context inspection remains necessary.',['SGA1','Deligne1980WeilII'],['S08','S24'],'programs/check_calculations.py: unipotent matrix; proofs.md §2.')
